{
  "what": "The wire contract for the device's access status: the JSON the native bridges write (accessStatus) and Rust reads (adapter_device_calendar::read_device_access). Rust's test reads these samples, each key's effect shown by at least one of them, and mobile/scripts/check-ffi-bridges.mjs holds the key paths each bridge's accessStatus() writes to the key paths its platform's samples use, both ways. So a key renamed in a writer, in the reader or here fails a check instead of going silent.",
  "why": "Nothing compiles Swift outside the iOS release workflow, and the field case behind granted_this_run only shows on a phone: iOS stated 'never asked' for the reminders a second after granting them, until Aperio was started anew (decision 187). A key Rust does not read is ignored without a word, so a misspelled granted_this_run would bring back exactly that case.",
  "writtenBy": [
    "mobile/modules/cal-ffi/ios/IosDeviceEventStore.swift (accessStatus)",
    "mobile/modules/cal-ffi/android/src/main/java/expo/modules/calffi/AndroidDeviceCalendar.kt (accessStatus)"
  ],
  "readBy": ["crates/adapter-device-calendar/src/lib.rs (read_device_access)"],

  "samples": [
